            <ArticleTitle>Alterations in response to somatic pain associated with anaesthesia. XXIII: Further study of naloxone.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>463-8</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Tibial pressure analgesimetry has been employed to study the antagonistic effects of small doses of naloxone in pethidine-induced analgesia. The dose of pethidine was 100 mg and the doses of naloxone 0.2, 0.1, 0.05 and 0.025 mg/60 kg body weight, and all drugs were given i.v. These doses of naloxone reduced the analgesia produced by pethidine and the degree of antagonism was probably dose-related. It was also found that naloxone antagonized the side-effects of pethidine, especially nausea.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
